cff-version: 1.2.0
message: >-
  If you use this software, please cite it using the metadata below. The data
  products a given run fetches carry their own citations, which are separate
  and also required - see the References section of the README, and the
  reference field each source catalog carries at runtime.
title: "datamatch: Fetch Ocean Data from Several Sources and Match It in Space and Time"
type: software
version: 0.2.0
license: MIT
repository-code: "https://github.com/chross22/datamatch"
url: "https://chross22.github.io/datamatch/"
abstract: >-
  Downloads ocean and atmosphere data and joins it to point data in space and
  time, at the downloaded data's own resolution. The join is a general
  spatiotemporal nearest-feature match, so either side may be species
  observations, survey stations, tag positions, or another gridded product.
  Five sources are covered behind one interface and one set of variable names -
  the Copernicus Marine Service, HYCOM, FVCOM, CCMP and NOAA ERDDAP - so a
  covariate arrives in a column of the same name whichever supplied it, and
  which source produced each value is recorded on the result. Also provides
  spatial and temporal resampling, gap filling for satellite ocean colour,
  seafloor terrain, basin-scale climate indices, plots for inspecting what was
  downloaded, and Ecological Metadata Language for a matched table.
authors:
  - family-names: Ross
    given-names: Camille
    email: camille.ross@maine.edu
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-1428-2294"
keywords:
  - R
  - oceanography
  - marine ecology
  - spatiotemporal matching
  - environmental covariates
  - Copernicus Marine Service
  - HYCOM
  - FVCOM
  - satellite remote sensing
  - species distribution modelling
